      "content": "Ah, I see! You're right to be confused, but I must clarify: there is **no actual goblin** in your policy, and I apologize if my previous response sounded like I was hiding a literal goblin in your JSON. That was a misunderstanding on my part; I was just using the \"goblin\" theme to describe the hidden complexity or the \"invisible monster\" that is often the culprit in AWS permission errors.\n\nSince you've checked the bucket policy and it looks fine, the \"goblin\" is likely hiding in one of these specific places:\n\n1.  **The Lambda Execution Role**: The IAM role attached to your Lambda function must have the `s3:PutObject` permission explicitly granted. If the role doesn't have this permission, the bucket policy won't matter, just like a goblin can't open a door if it doesn't have a key.\n2.  **The Bucket Policy Principal**: Ensure the `Principal` in the bucket policy matches the **ARN of the Lambda execution role** exactly. If there's a typo in the account ID or the role name, the \"goblin\" (the permission check) will fail silently.\n3.  **Bucket Versioning or",
